Good pushback on all four. Let me trace the actual control flow rather than paraphrase.

## When the fixup was implemented (not tonight)

`git log` shows the fixup itself landed in two commits on **2026-09-13**: `f32e9ef` ("Added Slice 1 of rb_delete") and `8fa9072` ("doubly-black delete fixup loop, completing rb_delete"), both before today. Since then it's passed `make test`/`asan`/`memcheck` and a 10^5-op fuzz run. Tonight's proposed work is *not* writing the algorithm — it's adding table rows that pin specific branches to specific hand-built trees. That distinction matters for exactly the reason you're raising: the fuzzer proves the algorithm survives ~100k random trees under asan/valgrind, but it doesn't tell us *which named case fired* on any given op, so a regression that breaks, say, Case 4's near-red conversion could hide behind the fuzzer's aggregate pass/fail for a long time before a random tree happens to hit it in a way that trips `rb_validate`. Table-driven tests convert "probably exercised" into "deterministically exercised, by name, every run." That's the legitimate scope of "test coverage" here. I should not have written "no changes needed" as if that settled correctness — it only means "these two slices already have that deterministic pinning; the other three don't yet." Below I trace the actual code instead of asserting it's right.

## Slice 1 — why the loop "re-enters" (it doesn't, that was my error)

```c
if (x == parent->left) {
    struct rb_node *sib = parent->right;
    if (is_red(sib)) {                     /* Case 5 */
        sib->color = BLACK;
        parent->color = RED;
        rotate_left(t, parent);
        sib = parent->right;
    }
    if (is_black(sib->left) && is_black(sib->right)) {   /* Case 3 check */
        ...
    } else {
        ...                                              /* Case 4 check */
    }
}
```

There's no `continue` and no jump back to `while`. After the Case-5 rotation reassigns `sib`, execution just **falls through**, still inside the same `while` iteration and the same `if (x == parent->left)` block, straight into the `is_black(sib->left) && is_black(sib->right)` test — now evaluated against the *new* sibling (`sib`'s old left child, which the RB invariants guarantee is black, since the old red `sib` can't have had a red child). So Case 5 is never terminal by itself: it's a reshuffle that guarantees the next check sees a black sibling, then that check decides Case 3 or Case 4 in the *same pass*. The only way control returns to the top of `while` is if that Case-3 branch runs and sets `x = parent` — i.e., only Case 3b (parent black, debt climbs) causes an actual new loop iteration. I stated this imprecisely as "loop re-enters" in the plan; the accurate statement is "falls through into the Case 3/4 check within the same iteration."

## Slice 2 — Case 4 is two distinct control paths, agreed

You're right to split this. Tracing the left-child branch (`rbtree.c:202–213`):

```c
} else {                                    /* sib is black, has >=1 red child */
    if (is_black(sib->right)) {             /* far nephew black => near nephew must be red */
        if (sib->left != NULL) sib->left->color = BLACK;
        sib->color = RED;
        rotate_right(t, sib);
        sib = parent->right;
    }
    sib->color = parent->color;             /* now unconditionally: far nephew red */
    parent->color = BLACK;
    if (sib->right != NULL) sib->right->color = BLACK;
    rotate_left(t, parent);
    return;
}
```

Two genuinely different paths through this:
- **Far nephew red directly** (`sib->right` is red, regardless of `sib->left`): skips the `if (is_black(sib->right))` block entirely, goes straight to the 4-line terminal recolor+rotate.
- **Near nephew red, far nephew black**: `is_black(sib->right)` is true, so it *first* executes the conversion (`sib->left->color = BLACK`, `sib->color = RED`, `rotate_right(t, sib)`, reassign `sib`), *then* falls into the same terminal block, now operating on the newly-rotated sibling.

These exercise different code (`rotate_right(t, sib)` only fires in the second path) and deserve separate table rows, each with a mirror — so Slice 2 becomes 4 cases, not 2: far-red-direct (left), far-red-direct (right mirror), near-red-conversion (left), near-red-conversion (right mirror).

## Case 3a — exactly where does parent turn black?

Not inside the both-nephews-black branch. Trace:

```c
if (is_black(sib->left) && is_black(sib->right)) {  /* Case 3 */
    sib->color = RED;      /* <- only write in this branch */
    x = parent;
    parent = x->parent;
}
```
This branch never touches `parent->color`. It just recolors the sibling red and reassigns `x`/`parent` for re-evaluation. Then back at the `while` guard:
```c
while (parent != NULL && is_black(x)) { ... }
```
`x` is now the *old* parent node. In the Case-3a scenario (old parent was red), `is_black(x)` is **false**, so the loop condition fails and the body does **not** run again — the loop exits immediately. Falling out of the loop:
```c
if (x != NULL) x->color = BLACK;   /* rbtree.c:243 */
```
This line — the loop's exit-time catch-all, not a branch inside Case 3 — is what actually paints the old parent black. So the "P absorbs the shared debt" step described in NOTES.md happens via the *while-condition-fails-then-fallthrough-to-line-243* path, not via an explicit `if (is_red(parent))` anywhere in the Case-3 code. (For Case 3b, the same line 243 is what terminates the climb when it reaches a black `x` — including the root, where `parent == NULL` ends the loop and line 243 recolors the root black, matching invariant 1.)

## Revised Slice 2 (replaces the version you're reviewing)

- **2a** — far nephew red directly, `x` is left child. Sequence TBD (need to hand-derive/verify via the instrumented-build technique used for the red-sibling case).
- **2b** — mirror of 2a (`x` is right child, far nephew = `sib->left` red).
- **2c** — near nephew red / far nephew black, `x` is left child (exercises the `rotate_right(t, sib)` conversion at line 206).
- **2d** — mirror of 2c.

Want me to proceed to derive and verify the insertion sequences for 2a–2d plus Slice 3 (Case 3a) and Slice 4 (Case 3b single- and multi-level climb, including climb-to-root) before writing any test code — or do you want to see the hand-derived trees for one slice at a time as we go?

